const e=[{id:"l-homicide",moduleId:"m04-criminal-law",title:"Homicide: the structure of sections 299 and 300",minutes:30,summary:"Culpable homicide is the big category, murder is a smaller category sitting inside it, and the exceptions push a case back down again.",plants:[],prepares:["ps-homicide-01"],reading:[{bookId:"yeo-morgan-chan-criminal-law",where:"the chapters on homicide and on the general exceptions"}],source:"Penal Code (Act 574) ss 299, 300 and Exceptions 1 and 4, 302, 304; PP v Visuvanathan [1978] 1 MLJ 159; Virsa Singh v State of Punjab AIR 1958 SC 465 (Supreme Court of India).",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"Read ss 299, 300, 302 and 304 in a current reprint of Act 574. The sentencing provisions were amended in 2023 and must not be quoted from memory. Confirm the Visuvanathan citation and look for a more recent Federal Court statement of the s 300(c) test.",plate:"lamp-blow",plateCaption:"One blow with something picked up in the moment. Limb (c) is the one that fits these facts, and the one students skip past.",readMinutes:11,sections:[{id:"sec-l-homicide-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Start from the section rather than a theory of killing; the Code defines its own offences.",h:"Start with the Code, not with a theory",body:[{t:"p",text:"A man dies after a fight outside a shop, and somebody is arrested that same night. What exactly should he be charged with, and who decides?"},{t:"p",text:"In Malaysia the answer sits in a book called the Penal Code. Every offence has its own section, and each section writes in the state of mind it needs — no more and no less."},{t:"p",text:"So the first move in any criminal problem is always the same. Find the section, read it, and do not reason from a general feeling about guilt."},{t:"p",text:"Unlawful killing shows this better than anything else in the Code. The structure is clean once you have seen it, and thoroughly confusing if you arrive with English categories in your head."}]},{id:"sec-l-homicide-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Three steps in order: culpable homicide under s 299, then murder under s 300, then any Exception.",anchor:"fig-l-homicide-0",h:"Three steps, always in this order",body:[{t:"p",text:"Section 299 says what **culpable homicide** is, which is the Code's name for unlawful killing. Section 300 then says when culpable homicide counts as **murder**, and the exceptions to section 300 say when it drops back down again."},{t:"rule",text:"Section 299: culpable homicide is causing death by an act done with the intention of causing death, or with the intention of causing such bodily injury as is likely to cause death, or with the knowledge that the act is likely to cause death.",source:"Penal Code, s 299"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: it is culpable homicide if the person meant to kill, or meant to cause an injury that was likely to kill. It is also culpable homicide if they knew that what they were doing was likely to kill."},{t:"p",text:"Culpable homicide is the big category and murder is a smaller category sitting inside it. Every murder is culpable homicide, but not every culpable homicide is murder."},{t:"p",text:"So never ask “is this murder or culpable homicide?” as though the two were rivals. Ask first whether it is culpable homicide at all, and then ask whether it is the kind that section 300 turns into murder."},{t:"diagram",kind:"flow",title:"The route through a homicide problem",caption:"Always in this order. Going straight to section 300 because someone died inverts the structure and usually picks the wrong limb.",alt:"Three steps: establish culpable homicide under s 299, then ask whether a limb of s 300 elevates it to murder, then whether an Exception brings it back down.",steps:[{label:"Culpable homicide?",note:"s 299"},{label:"Murder?",note:"one of the four limbs of s 300"},{label:"Any Exception?",note:"back down to culpable homicide"}]},{t:"p",text:"Every limb of the analysis assumes something that is easy to skip past. The prosecution must establish that the accused's own act caused the death, and where an intervening cause is genuinely arguable that question has to be answered separately."}]},{id:"sec-l-homicide-2",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"Four limbs make it murder, and (c) — an injury sufficient in the ordinary course of nature — does most of the work.",h:"The four ways it becomes murder",body:[{t:"example",text:"Rosli and Daud argue outside a kedai runcit late one evening. Rosli picks up a metal pipe lying by the drain and swings it once at Daud's head, and Daud dies that night. Rosli tells the police that he never meant to kill anybody."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Rosli swung the pipe once, and he tells the police he never meant to kill anybody. Before you read on — does that keep this out of murder?",options:["No — it can be murder if he meant to cause the wound he caused","Yes — murder means meaning to kill, and he did not","It turns on whether Rosli knew that a pipe could kill a man"],answer:0,reveal:"The second one. Hold on to the first answer, though. It is what the word murder means in ordinary speech, and the Code is about to take that meaning away from you. Section 300 has four routes in, and only one of them asks about an intention to kill. The third answer is the near miss. What Rosli knew matters under some limbs, and not under the limb that will catch him."},{t:"p",text:"Section 300 has four limbs, lettered (a) to (d), and any one of them turns culpable homicide into murder."},{t:"list",items:["(a) The act is done with the intention of causing death — that is, meaning to kill.","(b) The act is done meaning to cause an injury that the accused knows is likely to kill this particular person.","(c) The act is done meaning to cause the injury actually caused, and that injury is enough, in the ordinary course of nature, to cause death.","(d) The act is so obviously dangerous that it must in all probability cause death, or cause an injury likely to cause death. The accused had no excuse for running that risk."]},{t:"rule",text:"Section 300(c) is two questions: did the accused intend to inflict the injury actually inflicted, and is that injury objectively sufficient in the ordinary course of nature to cause death.",source:"Penal Code, s 300(c); PP v Visuvanathan [1978] 1 MLJ 159, applying Virsa Singh v State of Punjab AIR 1958 SC 465 (Supreme Court of India)"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: did you mean to cause the wound you caused, and would a wound like that normally kill?"},{t:"p",text:"The second question has nothing to do with what was in the accused's head, because it is answered by the doctor who examined the body. That is why limb (c) is the one the prosecution reaches for most often."},{t:"p",text:"Go back to Rosli. He meant to strike Daud's head with the pipe, and that is the injury Daud suffered, and a pathologist will say that a blow like that normally kills. Limb (c) is made out, and the fact that Rosli did not realise it could kill is simply not the question."},{t:"caution",text:"It is tempting to run limb (a) because a person died. On a single blow with something picked up in the moment, (a) is hard to prove and (c) is straightforward. Choosing the wrong limb means proving a harder case than the law asks for."}]},{id:"sec-l-homicide-3",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"Exceptions push murder back down: provocation, exceeding private defence, sudden fight.",h:"The exceptions: murder pushed back down",body:[{t:"p",text:"Suppose a limb of section 300 is made out on the facts. The offence is then murder, unless one of the exceptions to the section applies."},{t:"p",text:"The exceptions do not set anybody free, and that is the thing to hold on to. They reduce murder to culpable homicide not amounting to murder, which is a lesser offence carrying a lesser sentence. Treating provocation as a way out altogether is the commonest mistake in this area, because it is a reduction and not an escape."},{t:"rule",text:"Exception 1: culpable homicide is not murder if the offender, whilst deprived of the power of self-control by grave and sudden provocation, causes the death of the person who gave the provocation.",source:"Penal Code, s 300, Exception 1"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: you were pushed past self-control by something serious that had just happened, and you killed the person who did it."},{t:"p",text:"Two words carry all the weight and they fail in different ways. **Grave** asks how serious the provocation was, while **sudden** asks whether there was time for temper to cool."},{t:"example",text:"Halim's neighbour has insulted his late mother, on and off, for two years. One evening Halim takes a parang and kills him for it. The provocation may well be grave, but it is hard to call it sudden, because two years is a long time in which to cool down."},{t:"p",text:"An Explanation to the section makes whether provocation was grave and sudden a question of fact, and that is what allows words alone to count as provocation."},{t:"caution",text:"Three provisos cut the exception down, and any one of them will sink it. The provocation must not have been sought out or brought on by the offender himself. It must not come from something done in obedience to the law, or by a public servant acting lawfully. Nor may it come from somebody who is lawfully defending themselves."},{t:"p",text:"Exception 4 covers a sudden fight, where the killing happens in the heat of passion on a sudden quarrel. The offender must not take undue advantage or act in a cruel or unusual way, and the exception is worth checking on any facts involving a scuffle."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"Grave and sudden provocation is made out on the facts. What happens to the accused?",options:["He walks free — provocation is a complete defence","The offence drops to culpable homicide not amounting to murder","He is still convicted of murder, and only the sentence changes"],answer:1,why:"Exception 1 reduces the offence. It does not excuse the killing, and it does not merely soften the sentence. The first answer is the commonest mistake in this area, because the word *defence* sounds like a way out. The third is the other one. The conviction itself changes, from section 302 to section 304."}]},{id:"sec-l-homicide-4",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Sections 302 and 304 carry the punishments, and the sentencing law changed in 2023.",h:"Sentence",body:[{t:"p",text:"Section 302 punishes murder, and section 304 punishes culpable homicide not amounting to murder."},{t:"p",text:"Section 304 splits by state of mind, because the intention limb and the knowledge limb carry different maximum sentences. So the limb you settled on earlier is what decides which maximum applies."},{t:"caution",text:"The mandatory death penalty for murder was abolished in 2023, and sentencing is now at the court's discretion. Do not state a range from memory or from an older book — read the current provision. This is the single most likely thing on this page to have moved since it was written."}]},{id:"sec-l-homicide-5",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"One blow, a clear post-mortem, and arguable provocation — the problem is built to test exactly this.",h:"What to do with this",body:[{t:"p",text:"Work the problem question, which is built to test exactly this. It gives you a single blow, a clear pathologist's report, and provocation that is arguably grave and doubtfully sudden."},{t:"p",text:"The work is in taking section 300(c) as two separate questions, and then arguing both sides of suddenness rather than asserting one of them."}]}],quiz:[{id:"qhm1",q:"How do sections 299 and 300 fit together?",options:["They are two separate offences of equal weight","Section 300 sets the sentence for section 299","Culpable homicide is the big category; murder is a smaller one inside it","Section 299 applies only where section 300 does not"],answer:2,why:"Every murder is culpable homicide. Not every culpable homicide is murder.",source:"Penal Code ss 299 and 300"},{id:"qhm2",q:"Section 300(c) asks two questions. What are they?",options:["Did the accused intend death, and did death follow","Was the act very dangerous, and did the accused know it","Did the accused intend the injury actually caused, and is that injury enough in the ordinary course of nature to cause death","Was there provocation, and was it grave and sudden"],answer:2,why:"The second question is answered by the pathologist, not by the accused. That is why (c) is often easier to prove than (a).",source:"Penal Code s 300(c)"},{id:"qhm3",q:"One blow is struck with an object picked up in the moment, and the victim dies. Which limb is the natural one to run?",options:["(a)","(c)","(b)","(d)"],answer:1,why:"It is tempting to run (a) because someone died. On these facts an intention to kill is hard to prove, and (c) is straightforward.",source:"Penal Code s 300(c)"},{id:"qhm4",q:"What does Exception 1 to section 300 require?",options:["Grave and sudden provocation that destroyed self-control, and the death of the person who gave it","Any provocation at all","Provocation together with planning","Provocation given by someone else"],answer:0,why:"And it does not set the accused free. It reduces murder to culpable homicide not amounting to murder.",source:"Penal Code, Exception 1 to s 300"},{id:"qhm5",q:"What should you never state from memory about the sentence for murder?",options:["Which court tries it","The range — the mandatory death penalty went in 2023 and sentencing is now discretionary","How the offence is classified","The elements of the offence"],answer:1,why:"Sentencing provisions move. Always read a current source.",source:"Penal Code s 302; the sentencing provisions were amended in 2023 and must be read in a current reprint"}]},{id:"l-property-offences",moduleId:"m04-criminal-law",title:"Property offences: how possession was obtained",minutes:25,summary:"Theft, criminal breach of trust and cheating look alike from the outside. One question separates them, and it is not what was taken.",plants:[],prepares:["ps-property-01"],reading:[{bookId:"yeo-morgan-chan-criminal-law",where:"the chapters on offences against property"}],source:"Penal Code (Act 574) ss 378, 379, 405, 408, 415, 420.",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"Read ss 378, 405, 408, 415 and 420 in a current reprint of Act 574. The classification reasoning is settled; the section numbers for the aggravated and punishment provisions are what to confirm.",plate:"shop",plateCaption:"One counter, three routes out, three offences. The question is never what was taken but how possession was obtained.",readMinutes:10,sections:[{id:"sec-l-property-offences-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Three incidents in one shop on one afternoon, and three different offences.",h:"One Saturday in a hardware shop",body:[{t:"p",text:"Siti works the till at a small hardware shop in Klang, and three things happen there in one afternoon."},{t:"list",items:["A customer pays RM200 in cash, and Siti puts it straight into her own pocket and records no sale.","On her way out she takes a drill worth RM150 from the display shelf.","She tells the owner that a supplier needs RM300 up front, but there is no such supplier and she keeps the money."]},{t:"predict",prompt:"Three dishonest acts, one Saturday afternoon. Before you read on — which of them is theft?",options:["The drill only","All three — every time, she took something that was not hers","The drill, and the RM200 out of the till"],answer:0,reveal:"Only the drill. If you said all three, that is how the afternoon looks from outside, and it is the charge a hurried officer would bring. The Code does not sort offences by what was taken. It sorts them by how the property got into her hands, and Siti got hold of those three things in three different ways."},{t:"p",text:"All three are dishonest, and in all three the property belonged to somebody else. Yet these are three different offences under three different sections, and an officer who charges all of them as theft has got two of them wrong."}]},{id:"sec-l-property-offences-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"The question is never what was taken but how the accused came to have it.",anchor:"fig-l-property-offences-0",h:"The question that separates them",body:[{t:"p",text:"The tempting question is “what did she take?”, and that is the wrong question. The right one is how she came to have the property in the first place."},{t:"list",items:["Taken out of somebody else's possession, and without the consent of that person — that is theft.","Received lawfully, and then treated by her as though it were her own — that is criminal breach of trust.","Handed over willingly by its owner, but only because she lied to him — that is cheating."]},{t:"p",text:"Who owns the thing is not the test, because in all three of them the property belongs to somebody else. That common feature is what makes each of them an offence, and possession and consent are what tell the three apart."},{t:"diagram",kind:"branch",title:"One question separates the three offences",caption:"Not what was taken, and not how much it was worth. How possession was obtained.",alt:"A question about how possession was obtained, branching three ways. Never given: theft under section 378, moved out of possession without consent. Given lawfully: criminal breach of trust under section 405, entrusted and then misappropriated. Given but by a trick: cheating under section 415, where delivery was induced by deception.",question:"How did the accused come to have the property?",branches:[{cond:"never given",label:"Theft",note:"s 378 — moved out of possession without consent"},{cond:"given lawfully",label:"Criminal breach of trust",note:"s 405 — entrusted, then misappropriated"},{cond:"given, but by a trick",label:"Cheating",note:"s 415 — delivery induced by deception"}]},{t:"p",text:"One further element runs through all three offences and is easy to overlook. Every one of them requires **dishonesty**, so an honest belief that you were entitled to the property is capable of answering any of the three charges."}]},{id:"sec-l-property-offences-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Theft under s 378: moved out of someone's possession, without consent, dishonestly.",h:"Theft",body:[{t:"rule",text:"Section 378: whoever, intending to take dishonestly any movable property out of the possession of any person without that person's consent, moves that property in order to such taking, commits theft.",source:"Penal Code, s 378"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: you take a thing that is not yours out of somebody else's hands, without their agreement, and you move it."},{t:"p",text:"Two elements do the work here: the property must come out of somebody else's possession, and it must be taken without the consent of the person who had it."},{t:"p",text:"Two smaller points matter as well, and both of them catch people out. Only movable property can be stolen, so land cannot be, and the offence is complete the moment the property is moved with a view to taking it. Getting away with the goods afterwards is not required at all."},{t:"example",text:"The drill was sitting on the shop's shelf and in the shop's possession, and nobody had handed it to Siti or put her in charge of it. Taking it off the shelf and walking out with it is therefore theft."}]},{id:"sec-l-property-offences-3",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Criminal breach of trust under s 405: it came to them lawfully, and was then misappropriated.",h:"Criminal breach of trust",body:[{t:"rule",text:"Section 405: whoever, being in any manner entrusted with property, or with any dominion over property, dishonestly misappropriates or converts it to his own use, or dishonestly uses or disposes of it in violation of any direction of law or legal contract, commits criminal breach of trust.",source:"Penal Code, s 405"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: the property was put into your hands for a purpose, and you kept it or used it as though it were your own."},{t:"p",text:"**Entrusted** means that the property was put into your hands to hold or to handle on somebody else's behalf."},{t:"p",text:"Here the accused starts out with the property lawfully, so nothing comes out of anybody else's possession, because she already had it herself. The wrong lies in keeping for herself, or using as her own, the thing she was trusted with."},{t:"example",text:"Siti was allowed to take payment from customers, so the RM200 reached her hands lawfully, in her job as cashier. Pocketing it is criminal breach of trust rather than theft, because nothing was taken out of anybody else's possession."},{t:"p",text:"Where the accused is a clerk or a servant, a heavier form of the offence applies, and that is the form that fits most employee cases."},{t:"caution",text:"Whether an employee has possession of what passes through their hands, or only custody of it, is a genuine question that turns on what exactly they were entrusted with. Being a cashier does not put the whole shop in Siti's hands, so argue the point rather than simply assuming it."}]},{id:"sec-l-property-offences-4",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Cheating under s 415: consent was given, but a deception induced it.",h:"Cheating",body:[{t:"rule",text:"Section 415: whoever, by deceiving any person, fraudulently or dishonestly induces the person so deceived to deliver any property to any person, commits cheating.",source:"Penal Code, s 415"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: you tell somebody a lie, and because of the lie they hand something over."},{t:"p",text:"Cheating is the odd one out, because here the victim agrees and the property is handed over willingly. What poisons the agreement is the lie that produced it, and the lie is the thing you have to prove."},{t:"example",text:"The owner handed Siti RM300 for a supplier who did not exist. He agreed to part with the money, but he agreed to it only because of the lie he had been told, and that is cheating."},{t:"p",text:"Where the lie leads to property being handed over, the punishment section is section 420 rather than the general one. Charging under the wrong punishment section is a real error and not a matter of style."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"Puan Lim hands her neighbour RM500 and asks him to pay her electricity bill. He spends it on himself instead. Which offence fits?",options:["Theft — he used money that was not his","Cheating — he must have lied to get hold of it","Criminal breach of trust — the money was put into his hands for a purpose"],answer:2,why:"The money reached him lawfully, and for a stated purpose, so nothing came out of anybody's possession. That is section 405. Theft is the trap, because spending someone else's money feels exactly like stealing it. Cheating fails too: nothing in the facts says a lie is what made her hand the money over."}]},{id:"sec-l-property-offences-5",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Take each incident separately and ask the one question again; a bundle of facts is not one charge.",h:"Facts that arrive in a bundle",body:[{t:"p",text:"Real cases come as one story with several incidents inside it, and it is tempting to label them all the same way. Take each incident on its own and ask the one question of each."},{t:"caution",text:"One set of facts can support more than one charge, and charging in the alternative is ordinary practice. Saying which charge is the better one, and why, is worth more than picking one and moving on."}]},{id:"sec-l-property-offences-6",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"The problem gives you an officer who wants to charge all three as theft, and he is wrong twice.",h:"What to do with this",body:[{t:"p",text:"The problem question gives you a cashier, three incidents on a single Saturday, and an investigating officer who wants to charge all three of them as theft. Only one of the three actually is."},{t:"p",text:"The work is in the reason, and the reason is one line long. How did the accused come to have the property in the first place?"}]}],quiz:[{id:"qpo1",q:"What single question separates theft, criminal breach of trust and cheating?",options:["How the accused came to have the property","What was taken","How much it was worth","Whether the accused gained anything"],answer:0,why:"Taken without consent. Received lawfully, then kept. Or handed over because of a lie.",source:"Penal Code ss 378, 405 and 415"},{id:"qpo2",q:"Property is received lawfully and then treated as the receiver's own. Which offence?",options:["Criminal breach of trust","Theft","Cheating","Extortion"],answer:0,why:"Section 405. Entrusted with the property, then dishonestly taking it for yourself.",source:"Penal Code s 405"},{id:"qpo3",q:"Which section defines theft?",options:["Section 378","Section 405","Section 415","Section 300"],answer:0,why:"Dishonestly moving movable property out of another's possession, without their consent.",source:"Penal Code s 378"},{id:"qpo4",q:"Someone hands over property, but only because they were lied to. Which offence?",options:["Cheating","Theft","Criminal breach of trust","Robbery"],answer:0,why:"Section 415. The victim consents, but the lie is what produced the consent.",source:"Penal Code s 415"},{id:"qpo5",q:"Can one set of facts support more than one charge?",options:["No, the charges rule each other out","Only with the court's leave","Yes, and charging in the alternative is ordinary practice","Only in the Sessions Court"],answer:2,why:"Saying which charge is the better one, and why, is part of the answer.",source:"Standard charging practice; charges may be laid in the alternative"}]},{id:"l-non-fatal",moduleId:"m04-criminal-law",title:"Offences against the person short of death",summary:"The Penal Code grades harm rather than treating all injury alike. The grading decides the offence, and the offence decides almost everything else.",minutes:28,readMinutes:9,reading:[{statuteId:"penal-code",where:"the chapter dealing with offences affecting the human body — read the definitions first, then the grading provisions, and note the section numbers yourself with the date you checked"}],source:"The structure of graded offences against the person is that of the Penal Code. No section number is stated, because this corpus has verified only Penal Code ss 6, 99(3) and (4), 299, 300, 378, 405 and 415, none of which are the provisions dealt with here.",verify:"Open the Penal Code as currently published by the Attorney General's Chambers, check the currency date on the front matter, and find the provisions yourself. This lesson deliberately names none.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",sections:[{id:"sec-l-non-fatal-0",h:"A ladder of harm",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"The Code sorts injury by seriousness, so identifying the grade is the first analytical step.",body:[{t:"p",text:"The homicide lesson showed a structure: a wide category, a narrower one inside it, exceptions pushing a case back down."},{t:"p",text:"Offences short of death are organised on a similar principle. Harm is graded, and the grade you are on decides which offence is charged."},{t:"p",text:"So the first question is not whether the accused did something wrong. It is how serious the harm was, in the Code's own terms."},{t:"caution",text:"The Code's categories are defined terms, not ordinary English. A word like hurt has a definition in the statute, and reasoning from the everyday meaning will put you on the wrong rung."}]},{id:"sec-l-non-fatal-1",h:"Harm, and the aggravating features",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"Offences are aggravated by the means used, the intention behind them, and the circumstances of the act.",body:[{t:"p",text:"Above the base grade the Code adds aggravating features, and they are worth knowing as categories."},{t:"list",items:["The seriousness of the injury itself","The means used — whether a weapon or dangerous instrument was involved","The intention or knowledge with which it was done","The circumstances, including who the victim was and why"]},{t:"p",text:"Each feature typically has its own provision, so an accurate answer names the offence that matches the features present rather than the most serious one available."},{t:"rule",text:"Section 320 defines grievous hurt exhaustively, as eight listed kinds. They include permanent privation of sight or hearing, fracture or dislocation, and hurt causing severe bodily pain or incapacity for ten days.",source:"Penal Code, ss 319-320"},{t:"rule",text:"Punishment for simple hurt is up to one year and/or a fine; for grievous hurt, up to seven years plus a fine. Each is aggravated separately where a dangerous weapon or means is used.",source:"Penal Code, ss 323, 325 (aggravated forms: ss 324, 326)"},{t:"rule",text:"Assault and criminal force are dealt with separately from hurt, in their own chapter. Assault is a gesture or preparation causing apprehension of imminent criminal force — words alone are not enough.",source:"Penal Code, ss 349-352"},{t:"predict",prompt:"Two accused cause identical injuries; one used a weapon. Same offence?",options:["Yes — the harm is what matters","No — the means used is a separate aggravating feature with its own provision"],answer:1,reveal:"Grading by outcome alone would make the Code much shorter and much blunter. The separate provisions exist precisely so that identical harm caused in different ways can be charged differently."}]},{id:"sec-l-non-fatal-2",h:"The mental element still has to be found",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Serious injury does not by itself establish the intention or knowledge the offence requires.",body:[{t:"p",text:"It is tempting to reason backwards from a bad injury to a guilty mind. The Code does not permit it."},{t:"p",text:"Each offence states its own mental element, and it has to be established on the evidence like anything else."},{t:"p",text:"So an answer must identify the mental element for the specific provision it relies on. It then has to apply that element to the facts, rather than inferring it from the harm."},{t:"p",text:"The general exceptions in Chapter IV apply here as they do elsewhere, and the burden on them sits with the accused."}]},{id:"sec-l-non-fatal-3",h:"Reading the provisions for yourself",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"The provisions above are now verified against the official reprint; the habit of checking still matters more than the number.",body:[{t:"p",text:"Earlier versions of this lesson named none. Nobody working on this corpus had checked them against the published Act, and an unchecked citation on a law site is worse than none."},{t:"p",text:"They are here now because someone read the current official reprint directly. That reading was cross-checked against an earlier reprint and the most recent amendment act, and confirmed the numbers had not moved."},{t:"rule",text:"As at 31 May 2023, the Penal Code's provisions on hurt, grievous hurt, and criminal force and assault sit where stated above, unaffected by the amendments since.",source:"Penal Code (Act 574), AGC updated text as at 31 May 2023"},{t:"p",text:"A number tells you where to look, not what you will find there. Read the definitions in full, in the chapter dealing with offences affecting the human body, before relying on this summary in an answer."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"A citation has appeared in a lesson that previously had none. What changed?",options:["Someone checked the section numbers against the current official reprint before writing them in — the rule was never that provisions go uncited, only that they go unverified","The rule about citing only verified provisions was relaxed for this lesson"],answer:0,why:"A wrong section number on a law site looks exactly like a right one and survives every automated check. The bar was always verification, not absence — and a citation that appears where one did not before should make you ask what changed, not assume the standard slipped."}]}],quiz:[{id:"q-crim-nf-1",q:"What decides which offence against the person is charged?",options:["The victim's opinion","The length of the trial","The grade of harm, together with aggravating features such as means and intention","Whether the accused has previous convictions"],answer:2,why:"The Code grades harm and then adds separate provisions for aggravating features. Matching the facts to the right combination is the analysis."},{id:"q-crim-nf-2",q:"Why can the mental element not be inferred from the seriousness of the injury?",options:["Because injuries are hard to assess","Because each offence states its own mental element, which must be established on the evidence","Because intention is presumed in all cases","Because the burden lies on the accused"],answer:1,why:"Reasoning backwards from harm to state of mind skips the element the provision actually requires. Serious harm can be caused with a mental state the charged offence does not cover."}]},{id:"l-inchoate-participation",moduleId:"m04-criminal-law",title:"Reaching people who did not do it themselves",summary:"Criminal liability extends to those who tried, those who helped, and those who acted together. Each is a different route and they are not interchangeable.",minutes:28,readMinutes:9,reading:[{statuteId:"penal-code",where:"the provisions on abetment, on common intention, and on attempt — find each, read it, and record the section numbers with the date you checked"}],source:"Attempt, abetment and common intention as structured by the Penal Code. No section number is stated, because this corpus has not verified the relevant provisions.",verify:"Read the abetment, common intention and attempt provisions in the current published Penal Code and check the reprint's currency date. This lesson names no section on purpose.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",sections:[{id:"sec-l-inchoate-participation-0",h:"Three routes, not one",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Attempt, abetment and common intention extend liability in different directions and require different things.",body:[{t:"p",text:"Most offences describe someone doing something. The Code also reaches people who did not."},{t:"p",text:"There are three distinct routes and they are commonly muddled."},{t:"list",items:["Attempt — the offence was not completed","Abetment — someone helped, instigated or conspired","Common intention — several acted together in furtherance of a shared intention"]},{t:"p",text:"They are not degrees of the same thing. An answer that says the accused was involved without naming the route has not identified the offence."}]},{id:"sec-l-inchoate-participation-1",h:"Attempt: past preparation",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"An attempt requires an act beyond mere preparation, together with the intention for the full offence.",body:[{t:"p",text:"Attempt asks how far along the accused had got."},{t:"p",text:"Thinking about an offence is not an attempt. Buying what you need is usually still preparation."},{t:"p",text:"Somewhere after that the conduct becomes an attempt, and the line is the hard part — it is a question of fact argued on the specifics."},{t:"p",text:"The mental element is demanding: an attempt generally requires the intention to commit the full offence, even where the full offence could be committed with something less."},{t:"rule",text:"The general attempt provision applies only where no other section already punishes the attempt at that particular offence. It caps the sentence at one-half of the longest term available for the completed offence.",source:"Penal Code, s 511"},{t:"predict",prompt:"Why might attempt require a higher mental element than the completed offence?",options:["It does not — the elements are always identical","Because without the result, intention is what identifies the conduct as criminal at all"],answer:1,reveal:"When the harm has not happened, the act on its own may be entirely innocent. What makes buying an ordinary object into an attempt is the purpose behind it, so intention carries more weight than it does when a result has occurred."}]},{id:"sec-l-inchoate-participation-2",h:"Abetment and common intention are different",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"Abetment is about helping or instigating another's offence; common intention is about acting together on a shared plan.",body:[{t:"p",text:"These two are confused more than any other pair in this area."},{t:"p",text:"Abetment looks at what one person did in relation to another's offence — instigating it, helping it, agreeing to it."},{t:"p",text:"Common intention looks at a group acting in furtherance of an intention they shared, and can make each liable for what was done in furtherance of it."},{t:"rule",text:"Abetment is defined as instigating a thing, engaging with others in a conspiracy to do it with an act done in pursuance of it, or intentionally aiding the doing of it. An abettor who abets an act that is in fact committed, with no separate punishment provided for the abetment itself, is punished as if he had committed the offence.",source:"Penal Code, ss 107-109"},{t:"rule",text:"Where a criminal act is done by several persons in furtherance of a common intention, each is liable for that act. It is as if he had done it alone.",source:"Penal Code, s 34"},{t:"p",text:"The practical difference is what you must prove. For abetment, the act of assistance or instigation. For common intention, the shared intention and participation in furtherance of it."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"Your accused drove others to a location knowing what they planned. Which route do you analyse?",options:["Both — driving may be an act of assistance, and there may also be a shared intention; they require different proof","Common intention only"],answer:0,why:"The same facts can support either or both, and they are established differently. Choosing one without considering the other risks running the route the evidence supports least."}]},{id:"sec-l-inchoate-participation-3",h:"Where the sections are, now that they are checked",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"The three routes now carry their own provisions, each verified against the current official reprint.",body:[{t:"p",text:"As with the last lesson, no section numbers used to appear here. This project cites a provision only once someone has checked it against the published Act, and nobody had."},{t:"rule",text:"As at 31 May 2023, attempt, abetment and common intention sit at the sections stated above. That is unaffected by the amendments since, including a 2025 amendment act checked and confirmed to touch none of them.",source:"Penal Code (Act 574), AGC updated text as at 31 May 2023"},{t:"p",text:"Read each provision in full before applying any of it to facts. Attempt sits in the chapter on attempts, abetment in its own chapter, common intention among the Code's general explanations."}]}],quiz:[{id:"q-crim-inch-1",q:"What distinguishes an attempt from preparation?",options:["The time elapsed","Whether a weapon was bought","Whether anyone was harmed","An act going beyond preparation towards commission, with the intention to commit the full offence"],answer:3,why:"Preparation is not criminal as an attempt. The question is whether conduct has moved beyond it, and that is argued on the specific facts rather than settled by a formula."},{id:"q-crim-inch-2",q:"How does abetment differ from common intention?",options:["Abetment concerns helping or instigating another's offence; common intention concerns acting together in furtherance of a shared intention","They are the same offence","Abetment applies only to property offences","Common intention requires a written agreement"],answer:0,why:"They reach different conduct and require different proof. The same facts may support both, which is why each has to be analysed rather than assumed."}]},{id:"l-sentencing",moduleId:"m04-criminal-law",title:"After conviction: what a court may impose",summary:"Conviction settles liability, not consequence. Sentencing is a separate exercise with its own purposes, its own material and its own arguments.",minutes:26,readMinutes:8,reading:[{statuteId:"criminal-procedure-code",where:"the provisions governing sentence — find what a court may impose and any procedural requirements, and note the sections yourself"},{bookId:"janab-criminal-procedure",where:"the treatment of sentencing practice, for how the factors are actually argued"}],source:"The purposes of sentencing and the standard aggravating and mitigating factors are general criminal law doctrine. Statutory limits are set by the Penal Code and the Criminal Procedure Code; no section, penalty or minimum is stated here, because this corpus has verified none of them.",verify:"Check what the statute permits for the specific offence in the current published Penal Code and Criminal Procedure Code, including any mandatory minimum, before relying on anything in this lesson to advise on outcome.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",sections:[{id:"sec-l-sentencing-0",h:"A separate stage",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Sentencing follows conviction as a distinct exercise, argued on different material.",body:[{t:"p",text:"A criminal case does not end at conviction. It moves to a stage with its own rules."},{t:"p",text:"The material differs. Evidence of the offence is settled; what matters now is the offender, the circumstances, and anything urged in mitigation."},{t:"p",text:"So the advocacy differs too. An argument that the accused did not do it has no place here, and running it wastes the one opportunity to affect the outcome."},{t:"caution",text:"Students routinely treat sentencing as an afterthought. For a convicted client it is the entire remaining question."}]},{id:"sec-l-sentencing-1",h:"Purposes that pull in different directions",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"Punishment, deterrence, protection and rehabilitation can point to different sentences on the same facts.",body:[{t:"p",text:"Sentencing serves several purposes at once and they do not agree."},{t:"list",items:["Retribution — a penalty proportionate to what was done","Deterrence — of this offender, and of others","Protection of the public","Rehabilitation of the offender"]},{t:"p",text:"On the same facts, deterrence may argue for a heavier sentence than retribution would allow, and rehabilitation for a lighter one than protection would permit."},{t:"predict",prompt:"A first offender commits an offence that is becoming common in the area. Which purposes conflict?",options:["Deterrence argues for severity to address prevalence; rehabilitation and proportionality argue against punishing this individual for a trend","None — they all point the same way"],answer:0,reveal:"This is the sharpest recurring conflict in sentencing, and it is where general deterrence is most criticised: it uses one offender to send a message to others. Naming the conflict is a better answer than pretending the purposes align."}]},{id:"sec-l-sentencing-2",h:"Aggravation and mitigation",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Factors are grouped around the offence and the offender, and the second is where mitigation usually lies.",body:[{t:"p",text:"Factors sort into two families and it helps to keep them apart."},{t:"p",text:"Those concerning the offence: planning, the harm caused, the victim's vulnerability, the use of a weapon, the offender's role."},{t:"p",text:"Those concerning the offender: previous record or its absence, age, a plea and when it was entered, cooperation, personal circumstances."},{t:"p",text:"A plea in mitigation that only asserts the offence was not serious is arguing against the conviction. The stronger material is usually in the second family."}]},{id:"sec-l-sentencing-3",h:"What limits the court",readMinutes:1,keypoint:'Chapter III of the Penal Code, headed "Punishments", contains no general statement of sentencing purpose — that material is common law, not statute.',body:[{t:"p",text:"A court is not at large. Statute sets what may be imposed for an offence, and sometimes fixes a minimum."},{t:"rule",text:'Chapter III of the Penal Code is headed "Punishments" (ss 53-75A), but most of it has been repealed. The original general provisions on kinds of punishment are gone. What remains is narrow. There is how a life sentence is treated for fraction calculations, a rule against double punishment for one act, which punishment applies where it is unclear which offence was committed, and doubled or mandatory-minimum sentences for repeat offenders in specified chapters.',source:"Penal Code, ss 57, 71, 72, 75, 75A"},{t:"p",text:"Retribution, deterrence, protection and rehabilitation are common law doctrine. They were developed by the courts rather than enacted by Parliament. That is exactly why the last section could state them without a section number, and why one will not appear for them here."},{t:"p",text:"The specific range for an offence — the maximum, and any mandatory minimum — sits with that offence's own punishment provision, not in Chapter III. Sentencing procedure and a court's powers on conviction sit in the Criminal Procedure Code, which this lesson has not checked and does not cite."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"Where does a court find the purposes of sentencing it is meant to balance?",options:["Nowhere in the Penal Code — they are common law, not a statutory list","Chapter III of the Penal Code, which lists them"],answer:0,why:"Checking Chapter III directly turned up no general sentencing-purposes provision — most of the chapter is repealed, and what remains is a handful of narrow technical rules. An answer that cites a Penal Code section for 'the purposes of sentencing' is citing something that is not there."}]}],quiz:[{id:"q-crim-sent-1",q:"Which sentencing purposes most often conflict?",options:["Retribution and protection","Rehabilitation and protection","General deterrence and proportionality to this offender's conduct","None conflict"],answer:2,why:"General deterrence looks outward to other potential offenders, while proportionality looks at what this offender did. Prevalence of an offence is the situation where the tension is sharpest."},{id:"q-crim-sent-2",q:"What is the weakest form of plea in mitigation?",options:["One relying on the offender's personal circumstances","One that in substance disputes the conviction","One mentioning an early plea","One raising the offender's age"],answer:1,why:"Liability is settled by then. Re-arguing it spends the one chance to influence sentence on a question the court has already decided."}]},{id:"l-fault-ladder",moduleId:"m04-criminal-law",title:"Four grades of fault, from intention down to negligence",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"The Penal Code does not use one test for a guilty mind. It uses four graded standards, and every offence names the one it needs.",source:"Penal Code (Act 574), s 299, for the intention and knowledge limbs. The rashness and negligence offences sit in the Code's chapter on offences affecting the human body; no section number is stated for them, because this corpus has verified only ss 6, 99(3) and (4), 299, 300, 378, 405 and 415.",verify:"This lesson states no section number for rash or negligent acts causing death or hurt on purpose; confirm it in the current Penal Code as published by the Attorney General's Chambers and record the date you checked. Read s 299 in the same reprint and check the currency date on its front matter.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{statuteId:"penal-code",where:"section 299 first, for the intention and knowledge limbs side by side, then the chapter on offences affecting the human body for the rash and negligent act offences — note those section numbers yourself with the date you checked"}],sections:[{id:"sec-l-fault-ladder-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Every offence names its own fault standard, so read the words before you argue about blame.",h:"One lorry, four states of mind",body:[{t:"p",text:"Encik Zainal drives a lorry along the Federal Highway. He clips a motorcyclist, who dies at the roadside."},{t:"p",text:"Everyone agrees on what his lorry did. The whole argument is about what was in his head."},{t:"p",text:"That argument has four possible answers here. They are not shades of one idea. They are separate standards, and the Penal Code names each of them."},{t:"list",items:["**Intention** — he meant that result to happen.","**Knowledge** — he did not want it, but he saw it coming as likely.","**Rashness** — he saw the risk and ran it anyway.","**Negligence** — he never saw a risk that a careful driver would have seen."]},{t:"p",text:"The four sit in order, from the most demanding down to the least. Moving down the ladder makes the prosecution's job easier."},{t:"caution",text:"Do not start from how bad the conduct feels. Start from the words of the section, because the section has already picked its standard for you."}]},{id:"sec-l-fault-ladder-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Intention is about what you wanted; knowledge is about what you saw coming.",h:"The top two rungs",body:[{t:"p",text:"The Penal Code, s 299, sets the top two rungs side by side. It is worth reading for that alone."},{t:"rule",text:"Section 299 reaches a person who causes death with the knowledge that the act is likely to cause death, as well as a person who intends death or intends an injury likely to cause it.",source:"Penal Code, s 299"},{t:"p",text:"Notice what that knowledge limb does. It catches a person who never wanted anybody to die."},{t:"p",text:"Knowledge asks what this accused actually foresaw. It is a real state of mind, and it has to be proved like any other."},{t:"example",text:"Zainal overtakes on a blind bend at speed. He says he hoped nothing was coming the other way. If he in fact saw that a crash was likely, that is knowledge."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Zainal insists he never wanted anyone hurt. Does that answer a charge built on knowledge?",options:["Yes — without a wish for the result there is no fault","Only if he also kept within the speed limit","No — knowledge asks what he foresaw, not what he wanted"],answer:2,reveal:"Wanting a result and foreseeing one are different questions. The second answer is the everyday meaning of a guilty mind, and the Code does not use it. The third is the near miss, because keeping to a speed limit does not stop a driver foreseeing a crash."}]},{id:"sec-l-fault-ladder-2",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Rashness runs a risk you actually saw; negligence misses a risk you ought to have seen.",h:"The lower two rungs",body:[{t:"p",text:"Below knowledge sit rashness and negligence. The Penal Code uses both words, and they do not mean the same thing."},{t:"p",text:"A rash person turns their mind to the risk. They see it, and they go ahead anyway, hoping it will not come off."},{t:"p",text:"A negligent person never turns their mind to it at all. They simply fall short of the care a reasonable person would take."},{t:"rule",text:"The Penal Code creates separate offences for causing death, and for causing hurt, by an act done rashly or negligently, and it treats the rash act as the graver of the two.",source:"Penal Code, the provisions on rash and negligent acts in the chapter on offences affecting the human body; section number deliberately not stated — see this lesson's verify note."},{t:"caution",text:"This lesson gives you no section number for those offences. Look them up in the Code yourself, and write down the date you checked."}]},{id:"sec-l-fault-ladder-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Match the facts to the standard the section names, then argue the rung above and the rung below.",h:"Putting the ladder to work",body:[{t:"table",caption:"The four standards, and the question each one asks",columns:["Standard","The question it asks","Where it fails"],rows:[["Intention","Did he mean this result?","He meant something else entirely"],["Knowledge","Did he foresee it as likely?","He never turned his mind to it"],["Rashness","Did he see the risk and run it?","He never saw the risk at all"],["Negligence","Would a careful person have seen it?","A careful person would have done the same"]],alt:"Four fault standards in a table. Intention asks whether he meant the result, and it fails if he meant something else. Knowledge asks whether he foresaw the result as likely, and it fails if he never turned his mind to it. Rashness asks whether he saw the risk and ran it, and it fails if he never saw the risk. Negligence asks whether a careful person would have seen the risk, and it fails if a careful person would have done the same."},{t:"p",text:"In an answer, name the standard the section uses. Then test the facts against it, and say what the next rung down would need."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"A charge requires knowledge. The evidence shows only that a careful person would have seen the danger. What follows?",options:["The charge fails on that element, though an offence built on negligence may still fit","The charge is made out, because the danger was obvious","Knowledge is presumed once the danger is obvious"],answer:0,why:"Obviousness supports negligence. It does not by itself show that this accused foresaw anything, and knowledge is about what he actually foresaw. The first and third answers both slide from what a reasonable person would see to what this person did see."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-general-explanations",moduleId:"m04-criminal-law",title:"The Code defines its own words",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"Ordinary English will not tell you what dishonestly or good faith mean in the Penal Code. The Code defines them itself, in a chapter near the front that governs every offence after it.",source:"Penal Code (Act 574), s 6, with ss 378, 405 and 415 as illustrations of the defined word dishonestly. No section number is stated for the definitions themselves, because this corpus has verified only ss 6, 99(3) and (4), 299, 300, 378, 405 and 415.",verify:"This lesson states no section number for the definitions of dishonestly, wrongful gain, wrongful loss, good faith, voluntarily, act and reason to believe on purpose; confirm it in the current Penal Code as published by the Attorney General's Chambers and record the date you checked.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{statuteId:"penal-code",where:"the chapter of general explanations near the front — read the defined terms in order, then section 6, and note each definition's section number yourself with the date you checked"}],sections:[{id:"sec-l-general-explanations-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A word in the Code can look ordinary and still carry a meaning the Code has fixed for it.",h:"A bicycle, and an honest mistake",body:[{t:"p",text:"Puan Kalsom wheels a bicycle out of a rack at a flat in Cheras. It is not hers. It looks exactly like hers, and hers is missing."},{t:"p",text:"Theft under the Penal Code, s 378, requires her to have acted dishonestly. So did she?"},{t:"p",text:"You cannot answer that from ordinary English. Dishonestly is a defined term, and the Code fixes what it means."},{t:"p",text:"The same is true of a whole set of words. They sit together in the Code's chapter of general explanations, near the front."}]},{id:"sec-l-general-explanations-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Dishonesty here is tied to wrongful gain or wrongful loss, not to a general sense of bad character.",h:"Dishonestly means gain or loss",body:[{t:"p",text:"Start with the word that carries the most weight. It appears in theft, in criminal breach of trust and in cheating."},{t:"rule",text:"The Penal Code defines a person as acting dishonestly when the person acts with the intention of causing wrongful gain to one person, or wrongful loss to another. Wrongful gain and wrongful loss are themselves defined terms, turning on property a person is not entitled to get or is entitled to keep.",source:"Penal Code, the chapter of general explanations, the definitions of dishonestly, wrongful gain and wrongful loss; section number deliberately not stated — see this lesson's verify note."},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: dishonesty in this Code is about property moving where it should not."},{t:"p",text:"Go back to Kalsom. She believed the bicycle was hers, so she intended no wrongful gain and no wrongful loss."},{t:"p",text:"The element fails, and the charge fails with it. Her mistake did not make her careless. It made her not dishonest."},{t:"p",text:"The same defined word does the same work in s 405 and in s 415 of that Code."},{t:"caution",text:"An honest claim of right answers all three of those property offences. It is an argument about an element, and not a plea for sympathy."}]},{id:"sec-l-general-explanations-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Three more defined words change results quietly, because each reads differently from its everyday twin.",h:"Good faith, voluntarily, and acts that are omissions",body:[{t:"list",items:["**Good faith** — the Penal Code ties it to due care and attention. A belief held carelessly is not held in good faith, however sincere it is.","**Voluntarily** — a person causes a result voluntarily if they cause it by means they knew, or had reason to believe, were likely to cause it.","**Act** — a single word can cover a series of acts, and an illegal omission can count as an act.","**Reason to believe** — a lower bar than knowing. It asks what a person had cause to think, given what was in front of them."]},{t:"example",text:"Encik Ravi signs a delivery note without reading it. He says he believed the goods had arrived. Sincerity will not carry him if he took no care at all."},{t:"p",text:"Look at what the omission point does. A duty to act, broken by doing nothing, can put a person inside an offence written in terms of acts."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Does a sincere but careless belief count as good faith under the Penal Code?",options:["Yes — sincerity is what good faith has always meant","No — the Code's own definition brings in due care and attention","Only where the offence carries a light sentence"],answer:1,reveal:"The everyday meaning of good faith is honesty on its own. The Code adds care, so a belief nobody bothered to check can fail the test even when it is genuine. The third answer invents a rule about sentence that the definitions simply do not contain."}]},{id:"sec-l-general-explanations-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"These provisions sit at the front of the Code because they run through everything that follows them.",h:"Why the front of the Code is worth reading first",body:[{t:"p",text:"General explanations are easy to skip. They read like a dictionary, and the offences look far more interesting."},{t:"p",text:"Skipping them is how answers go wrong. A defined word decides an element, and an element decides the charge."},{t:"p",text:"Section 6 of the Penal Code belongs to that same part of it. It tells you that every definition of an offence is read subject to the general exceptions, even where the section never mentions them."},{t:"p",text:"So the front of the Code does two jobs at once. It fixes the vocabulary, and it attaches the exceptions."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"Where should you look first when a section uses a familiar word such as dishonestly or good faith?",options:["In a dictionary of ordinary English","In the Code's own chapter of general explanations","In a reported case on that offence"],answer:1,why:"The Code defines these words for itself, and its definition beats both ordinary usage and your instinct. Cases still matter, but they are interpreting the defined term rather than replacing it."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-force-and-fear",moduleId:"m04-criminal-law",title:"Robbery and extortion: what force adds",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"Force and fear turn an ordinary property offence into a graver one. Which offence you end up with depends on how the owner's will was overcome, and on when.",source:"Penal Code (Act 574), ss 378 and 415 as the base offences against property. The extortion and robbery provisions sit in the Code's chapter on offences against property; no section number is stated for them, because this corpus has verified only ss 6, 99(3) and (4), 299, 300, 378, 405 and 415.",verify:"This lesson states no section number for robbery or extortion on purpose; confirm it in the current Penal Code as published by the Attorney General's Chambers and record the date you checked. The same goes for gang-robbery and for the aggravated forms of both offences, which this lesson does not number either.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{statuteId:"penal-code",where:"sections 378 and 415 first, then the provisions on extortion and on robbery in the chapter on offences against property — note those section numbers yourself with the date you checked"}],sections:[{id:"sec-l-force-and-fear-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"The same handbag can be taken in three ways, and each way carries a different offence.",h:"Three grabs at a night market",body:[{t:"p",text:"Encik Ganesan runs a stall at a night market in Ipoh. Three things happen near it on one evening."},{t:"list",items:["A young man lifts a handbag off a stall and walks away before anybody notices.","He pulls a second bag from a woman's shoulder, shoving her hard to get it free.","He tells a trader that the stall will burn tonight unless RM500 is handed over, and the trader pays."]},{t:"p",text:"The property is the same kind in all three. The offences are not."},{t:"p",text:"The earlier property lesson asked one question: how did the accused come to have the thing? This lesson adds a second question."},{t:"p",text:"Was the owner's will overcome, and if so, by force or by fear? That is what separates the grave property offences from the plain ones."}]},{id:"sec-l-force-and-fear-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"In extortion the victim hands the property across, because a threat has put them in fear.",h:"Extortion: fear as the lever",body:[{t:"p",text:"Take the trader who paid RM500. He was not robbed of it in the ordinary sense, because he handed it across himself."},{t:"p",text:"He handed it across because he had been put in fear of injury. That fear is what the Penal Code aims at in extortion."},{t:"rule",text:"Extortion is committed by a person who intentionally puts another in fear of injury, and thereby dishonestly induces that person to deliver property, or to deliver anything signed or sealed that may be converted into a valuable security.",source:"Penal Code, the provision defining extortion; section number deliberately not stated — see this lesson's verify note."},{t:"p",text:"Now line that up against cheating, in s 415 of the same Code. There too the victim hands the property over willingly."},{t:"p",text:"The difference is the lever. Cheating works by a lie, and extortion works by fear."},{t:"table",caption:"Three ways an owner's will is overcome",columns:["Offence","How the property moves","What overcame the will"],rows:[["Theft, s 378","Taken out of possession","Nothing — consent was never given"],["Cheating, s 415","Handed over","A deception"],["Extortion","Handed over","Fear of injury"]],alt:"Three offences compared. Theft under section 378: the property is taken out of possession, and consent was never given at all. Cheating under section 415: the property is handed over, and a deception is what overcame the will. Extortion, whose section number this lesson does not state: the property is handed over, and fear of injury is what overcame the will."}]},{id:"sec-l-force-and-fear-2",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Robbery is not a fresh way of taking; it is theft or extortion done with force or fear at the time.",h:"Robbery is built on top of the others",body:[{t:"p",text:"Robbery in the Penal Code sits on top of the two offences above. You cannot reach it without finding one of them first."},{t:"rule",text:"Robbery is theft, or extortion, aggravated by the offender causing or attempting to cause death, hurt or wrongful restraint, or fear of any of these, in order to commit the offence or to carry away the property obtained by it.",source:"Penal Code, the provision defining robbery; section number deliberately not stated — see this lesson's verify note."},{t:"p",text:"Timing carries a lot of the weight. The force must be used in committing the taking, or in getting away with what was taken."},{t:"example",text:"The shove that freed the second handbag was force used in order to take it. That is robbery, and not merely theft."},{t:"caution",text:"Force used long afterwards, for some unrelated reason, does not turn an earlier theft into robbery. Ask when the force was used, and what it was used for."}]},{id:"sec-l-force-and-fear-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Name the base offence first, then say exactly what the force or the fear adds to it.",h:"Charging the right one",body:[{t:"p",text:"An answer that says robbery without identifying the theft underneath it has skipped a step. The grave offence borrows its elements from the plain one."},{t:"p",text:"So work in order. Find the base offence, look for force or fear, then check when that force or fear was used."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"A man snatches a phone from a table and runs. A bystander grabs him, and he punches the bystander to get away. Is that robbery?",options:["No, never — the theft was complete the moment he picked the phone up","Yes, automatically — any violence after a theft makes it robbery","It is arguable, because force used to carry away the property is inside the definition"],answer:2,why:"The definition reaches force used in carrying away the property, so the punch is capable of counting. The first answer seals the theft off at the moment of taking. The third goes too far the other way, because violence for some unrelated reason would not qualify."},{t:"p",text:"This lesson names no section for robbery or for extortion. Find both in the Penal Code, and record the numbers with the date you checked."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-forgery-documents",moduleId:"m04-criminal-law",title:"Forgery: when the document itself lies",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"Forgery is not simply writing something untrue on paper. It is making a document that lies about how it came to exist, and that difference decides the charge.",source:"Penal Code (Act 574), s 415, used only for the contrast with cheating. The forgery provisions, the definition of a false document and the offence of using a forged document as genuine sit in the Code's chapter on offences relating to documents; no section number is stated for them, because this corpus has verified only ss 6, 99(3) and (4), 299, 300, 378, 405 and 415.",verify:"This lesson states no section number for forgery, for making a false document, or for using a forged document as genuine on purpose; confirm it in the current Penal Code as published by the Attorney General's Chambers and record the date you checked.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{statuteId:"penal-code",where:"the chapter on offences relating to documents — read the definition of a false document first, then the forgery provisions and the offence of using one as genuine, and note the section numbers yourself with the date you checked"}],sections:[{id:"sec-l-forgery-documents-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A false statement and a false document are different things, and only one of them is forgery.",h:"A letter that was never signed",body:[{t:"p",text:"Nurul is a clerk at a small firm in Johor Bahru. A client needs a letter of support from her employer."},{t:"p",text:"She types the letter and signs her employer's name at the bottom. The letter goes out that afternoon."},{t:"p",text:"Now change one detail. Suppose she signs her own name instead, and writes something untrue inside the letter."},{t:"p",text:"Both letters mislead the person who reads them. Only the first one is a false document, and that is the distinction this lesson turns on."}]},{id:"sec-l-forgery-documents-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"A document is false when it lies about who made it, or when, or with whose authority.",h:"What makes a document false",body:[{t:"p",text:"Think about what a document tells a reader before anyone reads a word of it."},{t:"p",text:"It says who made it. It says roughly when, and it often says on whose authority."},{t:"p",text:"Forgery attacks that layer. The document tells a lie about its own making."},{t:"rule",text:"The Penal Code treats a document as false where a person dishonestly or fraudulently makes, signs, seals or executes it so that it appears to have been made by someone who did not make it, or at a time when it was not made, or where it is altered after execution without lawful authority.",source:"Penal Code, the provision on making a false document; section number deliberately not stated — see this lesson's verify note."},{t:"p",text:"Nurul's first letter fails on the first limb. It appears to have been signed by her employer, and he never signed anything."},{t:"p",text:"Her second letter passes that test. It really was made by her, and it says so on its face."},{t:"predict",prompt:"The second letter contains a plain lie. Is that forgery?",options:["No — the document is genuine, even though what it says is untrue","Yes — a document containing a lie is a false document","Yes, but only where somebody relied on it"],answer:0,reveal:"A lie written into a genuine document is a lie in a document. Forgery needs the document itself to be false about its own making. The second answer is the natural reading of the word, and it is the mistake this area is famous for."}]},{id:"sec-l-forgery-documents-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Making a false document is not enough on its own; the Code also asks what it was made for.",h:"The intent the offence needs",body:[{t:"p",text:"A false document made as a joke, or as a classroom exercise, is not yet forgery."},{t:"p",text:"The Penal Code asks about purpose. It looks for an intention of a particular kind, and the list is short enough to learn."},{t:"list",items:["To cause damage or injury to the public, or to any person","To support a claim or a title","To cause a person to part with property","To make a person enter into an express or implied contract","To commit fraud, or so that fraud may be committed"]},{t:"p",text:"That list explains why forgery so often sits beside a property charge. The false document is the tool, and the gain is the point."},{t:"table",caption:"Three things the Code keeps apart",columns:["Conduct","What is false","Offence"],rows:[["Signing another person's name to a letter","The document's own making","Forgery"],["Telling a lie so that money is handed over","The statement, not the document","Cheating, s 415"],["Putting a forged letter into use as genuine","The document, and the use made of it","Using a forged document"]],alt:"Three offences kept apart. Signing another person's name to a letter makes the document's own making false, and that is forgery. Telling a lie so that money is handed over makes the statement false rather than the document, and that is cheating under section 415. Putting a forged letter into use as genuine involves both a false document and the use made of it. That is a separate offence, and this lesson does not state its number."}]},{id:"sec-l-forgery-documents-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"The person who makes a false document and the person who uses it need not be the same person.",h:"Using it is its own offence",body:[{t:"p",text:"Forgery is complete once the false document is made. Nobody has to be deceived by it, and nobody has to lose anything."},{t:"p",text:"The Penal Code deals separately with a person who uses a forged document as genuine, knowing or having reason to believe that it is forged."},{t:"p",text:"So two people can be charged out of one letter. One of them made it, and the other put it to work."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"Nurul's client posts the letter to a bank, knowing whose signature is really on it. What is his position?",options:["He is a witness only, because he made nothing himself","He may be liable for using a forged document as genuine","He may be liable for forgery, because he is the one who benefited"],answer:1,why:"Using a forged document as genuine is a separate offence, and knowledge or reason to believe is what it turns on. He did not make the document, so the making offence does not fit him at all."},{t:"p",text:"This lesson numbers nothing but the cheating provision. Open the Penal Code at the chapter dealing with documents, and write the rest down yourself."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-general-exceptions",moduleId:"m04-criminal-law",title:"The general exceptions, and who has to prove them",minutes:25,summary:"Chapter IV of the Penal Code applies to every offence in it. If you want the benefit of an exception you must prove it yourself, and the standard is the balance of probabilities.",plants:["ms-exc-01","ms-exc-02","ms-exc-03","ms-exc-04","ms-exc-05"],prepares:[],reading:[{bookId:"yeo-morgan-chan-criminal-law",where:"the chapters on the general exceptions and on private defence"}],source:"Penal Code ss 6, 76-106, and Exception 2 to s 300; Evidence Act 1950 s 105.",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"Check the current text of ss 82 and 83 for the ages, and check the current Federal Court statement of the standard to which an accused must prove a general exception — the phrase used in the judgments matters more than the paraphrase above.",plate:"shield",plateCaption:"The shield of Chapter IV, and the weight of section 105 — the accused carries it, on the balance of probabilities.",readMinutes:9,sections:[{id:"sec-l-general-exceptions-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Chapter IV applies to every offence in the Code, whether or not the section repeats it.",h:"They apply to every offence",body:[{t:"p",text:"A man pushes a snatch thief off a motorbike and the thief is badly hurt. Has he committed an offence? The definition of the offence will not tell you. The answer sits in a different part of the Penal Code."},{t:"p",text:"Sections 76 to 106 are called the General Exceptions. They are not defences to particular offences. They apply to every offence in the Code, and to offences under other statutes so far as the Code's general provisions are made applicable."},{t:"p",text:"You will not find any of them mentioned in the section that defines the offence. Section 6 is the reason why."},{t:"rule",text:"Every definition of an offence in the Penal Code is to be understood subject to the exceptions in the Chapter entitled 'General Exceptions', though those exceptions are not repeated in the definition itself.",source:"Penal Code, s 6"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: every offence in the Code is read with the general exceptions attached, even though the definition never mentions them."},{t:"p",text:"So a homicide answer has looked at only half the picture if it discusses the exceptions to section 300 and never asks about private defence or unsoundness of mind."}]},{id:"sec-l-general-exceptions-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Mistake, accident, necessity, infancy, unsoundness of mind, intoxication, consent, private defence.",h:"The main groups",body:[{t:"list",items:["**Mistake and being bound to act (ss 76 and 79)** — a person bound by law to do the act. Or a person who, by a mistake of fact and in good faith, believes himself bound or justified by law. Mistake of *fact*, not of law.","**Judicial and official acts (ss 77 and 78)** — a judge acting judicially, and a person acting under a court's judgment or order.","**Accident (s 80)** — an accident or misfortune, without criminal intention or knowledge, while doing a lawful act by lawful means with proper care and caution.","**Necessity (s 81)** — an act likely to cause harm, done without criminal intention and in good faith, to prevent other harm to person or property.","**Infancy (ss 82 and 83)** — nothing done by a child under ten is an offence. Between ten and twelve it turns on whether the child had attained sufficient maturity of understanding.","**Unsoundness of mind (s 84)** — by reason of unsoundness of mind, the person was incapable of knowing the nature of the act. Or incapable of knowing that it was wrong or contrary to law.","**Intoxication (ss 85 and 86)** — narrow. It covers intoxication caused without the person's knowledge or against his will, and intoxication producing insanity.","**Consent (ss 87 to 92)** — with limits set by age and by the degree of harm.","**Private defence (ss 96 to 106)** — of the body, and of property."]}]},{id:"sec-l-general-exceptions-2",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"Private defence has limits: no more harm than necessary, and not where the authorities could be reached.",h:"Private defence",body:[{t:"p",text:"Private defence appears in more problems than any other exception. It is a matter of degree, so it is genuinely arguable. Two ideas hold it in place."},{t:"example",text:"Ah Meng is walking home when a man grabs his bag and shoves him. Ah Meng pushes back and the man falls. That is likely to be within the right. Now change one thing. The man drops the bag and walks off, and Ah Meng chases him and hits him with a helmet. The danger was over. That is a different case."},{t:"p",text:"Section 99 sets the outer edges of the right. It says when there is no right at all, and how far the right can ever go."},{t:"rule",text:"There is no right of private defence against an act which does not reasonably cause the apprehension of death or grievous hurt, if there is time to have recourse to the protection of the public authorities; and the right in no case extends to inflicting more harm than is necessary.",source:"Penal Code, ss 99(3) and 99(4)"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: if there was time to go to the authorities and no reasonable fear of death or grievous hurt, there is no right in the first place. And even where the right exists, it never licenses more harm than the situation needs."},{t:"p",text:"Section 100 sets out the cases in which the right of private defence of the body extends to causing death. They include a reasonable apprehension of death, and of grievous hurt, and certain enumerated offences. Section 103 does the same for property."},{t:"predict",prompt:"A man defends himself against an attack, goes further than the danger needed, and kills. He acted in good faith and had no plan. What does the excess do?",options:["Nothing — he went too far, so it is murder","It brings the conviction down to culpable homicide not amounting to murder","It leaves the murder conviction and only softens the sentence"],answer:1,reveal:"Going too far does not simply cancel the defence. Exception 2 to section 300 brings the charge down, so the excess changes the conviction and not only the sentence. If you chose the first answer, you were right that the defence did not stretch that far. The Code just does something else with the excess."},{t:"caution",text:"The commonest error is to treat the right as lost because the response was excessive. Excess goes to whether the right extended *this far*. An accused who exceeds the right, in good faith and without premeditation, may still bring the charge down. Murder becomes culpable homicide not amounting to murder, under Exception 2 to section 300. Those are different convictions and they carry different sentences."},{t:"diagram",kind:"branch",title:"Section 99 asks whether the right exists at all",caption:"Excess does not destroy a right that existed. It goes to whether the right extended this far. Answering the two questions in the wrong order is the commonest error in this area.",alt:"A question — was there a reasonable apprehension of death or grievous hurt, or no time to go to the authorities? — branching two ways. No: section 99 means no right arises, and the enquiry ends. Yes: the right exists, and the next question is how far it extends, with section 100 setting out when it reaches causing death.",question:"Reasonable fear of death or grievous hurt, or no time to reach the authorities?",branches:[{cond:"no",label:"No right arises",note:"s 99 — the enquiry ends here"},{cond:"yes",label:"The right exists — now ask how far",note:"s 100 lists when it reaches causing death"}]}]},{id:"sec-l-general-exceptions-3",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Section 105 puts the burden on the accused, on the balance of probabilities — not on the prosecution.",h:"Who has to prove it",body:[{t:"p",text:"The prosecution must prove the offence. But if you want the benefit of an exception, the work of proving it falls on you."},{t:"rule",text:"When a person is accused of any offence, the burden of proving the existence of circumstances bringing the case within any of the General Exceptions in the Penal Code is upon him, and the court shall presume the absence of those circumstances.",source:"Evidence Act 1950, s 105"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: the court starts by assuming the exception does not apply, and it is for the accused to show that it does."},{t:"p",text:"That is a legal burden on the accused, and it is discharged on the balance of probabilities — more likely than not. It is not the criminal standard."},{t:"p",text:"The prosecution's own burden is untouched by any of this. It must still prove the offence beyond reasonable doubt, and it carries that burden throughout."},{t:"example",text:"Rosli is charged after a fight and says he was acting in self-defence. He does not have to make the court sure of it. He has to make the court think it more likely than not. If he does, the exception is his."},{t:"caution",text:"Do not write that the accused must merely 'raise' the exception, and that the prosecution must then disprove it beyond reasonable doubt. That is the English position on some defences. It is not what section 105 says."},{t:"compare",title:"Two burdens, at two different standards",left:{h:"The prosecution — the offence",items:["Must prove every element of the offence.","Beyond reasonable doubt.","This burden is untouched by any exception, and it never shifts.","It carries that burden throughout."]},right:{h:"The accused — an exception (s 105)",items:["The court starts by assuming the exception does not apply.","A legal burden, on the accused, to show that it does.","On the balance of probabilities — more likely than not.","**Not** merely raising it and leaving the prosecution to disprove it. That is the English position, and it is not ours."]}}]}],quiz:[{id:"qge1",q:"Who has to prove a general exception, and to what standard?",options:["On the prosecution, beyond reasonable doubt","On the accused, beyond reasonable doubt","On the accused, on the balance of probabilities","It is a purely evidential burden"],answer:2,why:"Section 105 puts a legal burden on the accused and directs the court to presume the circumstances are absent.",source:"Evidence Act 1950, s 105"},{id:"qge2",q:"Section 79 of the Penal Code excuses an act done under a mistake of what?",options:["Law","Identity only","Fact","Either fact or law"],answer:2,why:"A mistake of fact, made in good faith, believing oneself justified by law. A mistake about the law does not excuse.",source:"Penal Code s 79"},{id:"qge3",q:"Under s 82, a child under what age cannot commit an offence at all?",options:["Ten","Seven","Twelve","Fourteen"],answer:0,why:"Nothing done by a child under ten is an offence. Between ten and twelve the question is sufficient maturity of understanding.",source:"Penal Code ss 82 and 83"},{id:"qge4",q:"An accused goes further than the right of private defence allowed, and kills. What is the likely effect?",options:["The right is simply lost and murder stands","It is a complete defence anyway","It goes only to sentence","Murder may be reduced to culpable homicide not amounting to murder"],answer:3,why:"Exception 2 to s 300 covers exceeding the right in good faith and without premeditation.",source:"Penal Code, Exception 2 to s 300"},{id:"qge5",q:"Under s 99(3), when is there no right of private defence?",options:["Whenever the attacker is unarmed","Whenever the police could be called","Whenever the accused struck first","Where there is time to go to the public authorities and no fear of death or grievous hurt"],answer:3,why:"Both halves matter. Time to seek protection is not enough on its own if death or grievous hurt is reasonably feared.",source:"Penal Code s 99(3)"},{id:"qge6",q:"Why does Chapter IV matter to a case about a killing?",options:["The exceptions apply to every offence in the Code, s 300 included","They apply only to offences against property","They replace the exceptions to s 300","They apply only in the Sessions Court"],answer:0,why:"Section 6 makes every definition of an offence subject to the general exceptions.",source:"Penal Code s 6"}]}];export{e as default};
